{"ts":"2026-10-10T12:07:46.812Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"tool","toolName":"editor","toolCallId":"toolu_016MhCzgh8TVLbUK6iFKy41g","output":{"query":"edit:C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\README.md","result":"Edited C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\README.md\n```diff\n-1: # KaagazCheck: कागज़ चेक\n-2: \n-3: The government checks your papers after you apply and rejects you months later. KaagazCheck checks them **before** you apply, in Hindi, and tells you exactly what to fix.\n-4: \n-5: > Work in progress (Cline AI Builder Hackathon). The full README (problem, architecture, how Cline was used) comes in the final task.\n-6: \n-7: ## Setup\n-8: Requires Node 22+.\n-9: \n-10: ```powershell\n-11: npm install\n-12: copy .env.example .env.local   # then fill in CLINE_API_KEY and CLINE_MODEL\n-13: npm run dev\n-14: ```\n-15: \n-16: ## Scripts\n-17: `npm test` · `npm run typecheck` · `npm run lint` · `npm run build`\n+1: # KaagazCheck (কাগজ চেক): Check before you apply\n+2: \n+3: > We help citizens catch preventable welfare-application mistakes **before** they submit their papers. AI reads the documents; a tested rule engine checks them; and a Cline agent maintains the rules as official guidelines change.\n+4: >\n+5: > We reduce preventable errors. We do **not** guarantee approval; the department decides.\n+6: \n+7: ## The problem\n+8: Eligible people in India often lose welfare benefits *after* applying, because their documents disagree with each other (a name spelled differently on Aadhaar, the bank passbook and the land record; an account not linked to Aadhaar; a missing certificate), or because a rejection arrives with no reason they can understand.\n+9: \n+10: - **Rajasthan, PM-KISAN (2019):** only about 23% of about 51 lakh registered farmers received the third instalment; officials cited names on applications not matching Aadhaar. ([Hindustan Times](https://www.pressreader.com/india/hindustan-times-st-jaipur/20191007/281586652343870))\n+11: - **Andhra Pradesh, PM-KISAN (LibTech India, 2022):** about 15.2 lakh farmers missed instalments and about ₹1,343 crore was pending; about half were held with \"action required by the state\" and no stated reason. ([LibTech report](https://libtech.in/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/‘PM-KISAN-Samman-Nidhi-AP-25th-January.pdf))\n+12: \n+13: Finding schemes is already served (myScheme, Jugalbandi). **Getting an application through without a preventable rejection is not.** More evidence: `docs/research.md`.\n+14: \n+15: ## The solution\n+16: A mobile web app in **Assamese (default) with an English toggle**:\n+17: 1. Pick a scheme: **PM-KISAN** (flagship), **Orunodoi 3.0** (Assam), **Widow Pension (IGNWPS)**.\n+18: 2. Add documents: photo (AI reads them), type them in, or use the fake demo samples A, B or C.\n+19: 3. Confirm anything the AI was unsure about (low-confidence fields are highlighted).\n+20: 4. Answer a few yes/no questions.\n+21: 5. Get **two separate verdicts**: *Eligibility* (likely eligible / not eligible / needs verification) and *Application readiness* (ready for review / action required / needs verification), each with what to fix, where to fix it (bank, Aadhaar centre, circle office, Gram Panchayat) and the source of every rule (verified or provisional).\n+22: 6. Print a cover sheet to carry to the office.\n+23: \n+24: Plus a **rejection decoder** (`/decode`): paste a rejection or payment-status message and get the likely reason and fix steps. The model only picks one known reason code; the steps are fixed text in code, and anything below 0.7 confidence is shown as \"needs verification\" with questions to ask at the office.\n+25: \n+26: ### 3-minute demo path\n+27: 1. `npm run demo:reset`, then `npm run dev`.\n+28: 2. `/` → PM-KISAN → **Use sample B** → the report shows *action required*: the land-record name is a mismatch and the bank name is only a possible match. Sample A is clean (*ready for review*, bank seeding still to verify); sample C (widow pension) is missing the death certificate.\n+29: 3. `/admin` → load the Orunodoi demo notice → **Propose** → watch the tool calls, the diff and the tests → **Publish**. The citizen app now shows ₹1,500 for October 2026 and ₹1,250 after it.\n+30: 4. `/decode` → paste \"Payment failed: Aadhaar not seeded with bank account\".\n+31: \n+32: ## Cline inside the product: the Rule Maintenance Agent (`/admin`)\n+33: Government rules change. Paste a scheme notice and a **`@cline/sdk` agent**:\n+34: 1. reads the current rule pack and the notice;\n+35: 2. proposes the smallest rule change, with provenance (source, clause, effective date);\n+36: 3. writes regression tests and runs them **together with every existing test** for that scheme;\n+37: 4. shows a readable before/after diff and the test results;\n+38: 5. waits for a **human to Publish**; then the citizen app uses the new version immediately, and old versions are kept.\n+39: \n+40: The agent works only through five tools (`getRuleSchema`, `getCurrentRule`, `proposeRule`, `runTests`, `saveProposal`), defined in `src/server/agents/ruleMaintenance.ts`. **Code, not the prompt, enforces the safety rules** (`src/core/rulePolicy.ts`, `src/server/rules.ts`): the version must go up; at least 3 new tests with unique ids; a rule may be marked \"verified\" only if it was already verified with the same source or its source URL appears in the notice (otherwise it is \"provisional\", which the engine turns into \"needs verification\"); no save unless all tests pass; Publish re-runs every check.\n+41: \n+42: Demo inputs: `docs/sample-guideline-ignoaps.md` (adds the Old Age Pension) and `docs/sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md` (a one-time ₹1,500 payment for October 2026, with ₹1,250 still the regular amount).\n+43: \n+44: Set `ADMIN_PASSCODE` in `.env.local` to protect `/api/admin/*` (header `x-admin-passcode`); without it, `/admin` shows a \"Demo mode: unprotected\" banner.\n+45: \n```","success":true},"durationMs":9}}
